<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lubombo Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lebombo Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lubombo Mountains are a long, narrow range of hills in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, running north to south along the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. Formed of volcanic rock, they separate the lowveld of Eswatini from the coastal plain of Mozambique. The Swazi kingdom grew up to their west and the Tembe kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a> to their east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lubombo-mountains.jpg" alt="Lubombo Mountains" width="320" height="124" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ad Meskens You are free to use this picture for any purpose as long as you credit its author, Ad Meskens. Example: © ... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mkuze_Ghost_Mountain_(vicinity)_09_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
